.TH "xrdp\-sesman" "8" "@PACKAGE_VERSION@" "xrdp team" ""
.SH "NAME"
xrdp\-sesman \- \fBxrdp\fR(8) session manager

.SH "SYNTAX"
.B xrdp\-sesman
\-\-kill
.br
.B xrdp\-sesman
\-\-reload
.br
.B xrdp\-sesman
\-\-help
.br
.B xrdp\-sesman
\-\-version
.br
.B xrdp\-sesman
[\-\-nodaemon] [\-\-dump\-config] [\-\-config /path/to/sesman.ini]

.SH "DESCRIPTION"
\fBxrdp\-sesman\fR is \fBxrdp\fR(8) session manager.
.br
It manages user sessions by authenticating the user and starting the appropriate Xserver.

.SH "OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-k\fR, \fB\-\-kill\fR
Kills running \fBxrdp\-sesman\fR daemon.
.TP
\fB\-r\fR, \fB\-\-reload\fR
Reloads running \fBxrdp\-sesman\fR daemon.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Output help information and exit.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Output version information and exit.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-nodaemon\fR
Starts \fBxrdp\-sesman\fR in foreground instead of starting it as a daemon.
.TP
\fB\-\-dump\-config\fR
Print the configuration on stdout before starting the daemon.
The default is not to do this.
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-config\fR
Specify a path to a different \fIsesman.ini\fR file. This option is intended
to be used primarily for testing or for unusual configurations.
.P
.RS
If you use this option, be aware that you will have to have a
\fB@sysconfdir@/@sysconfsubdir@/sesman.ini\fR in place too, as a few elements of
the system (notably \fBxrdp(8)\fR and \fBxrdp\-chansrv(8)\fR) will want
to read it.
.RE
.SH "SIGNALS"
.TP
\fBSIGHUP\fR
Causes \fBxrdp\-sesman\fR to reload its configuration. Needed if you're
not running \fBxrdp\-sesman\fR as a daemon.
.SH "SESSION ENVIRONMENT"
The environment variables listed below are passed to the session by
\fBxrdp-sesman\fR and \fBxrdp-sesexec\fR when a session is started.
.br
Additional variables can also be specified in \fIsesman.ini\fR. See
\fBsesman.ini(5)\fR for more details.
.br
Note that after a session is started, environment variables cannot be
changed after a client disconnects from, and reconnects to a session.
.br
Some variables used in the internal implementation of \fBxrdp\fR are not
documented below. The names or contents of these variables may change
between \fBxrdp\fR releases.
.TP
\fBXRDP_SESSION_CODEC\fR
Set before the session is opened, so PAM session modules can read it with
\fBpam_getenv\fR(3), and passed on to the session. It names the codec
\fBxrdp\fR negotiated with the client when the session was created:
\fIh264\fR, \fIrfx\fR or \fIjpeg\fR. It is not set for other clients,
or for sessions started with \fBxrdp\-sesrun\fR.
A PAM module could, for example, set \fBXRDP_XORG_CONFIG\fR to start the
X server without a GPU for a client that only uses RemoteFX, which is
encoded on the CPU.
.SH "FILES"
@sbindir@/xrdp\-sesman
.br
@bindir@/xrdp\-sesrun
.br
@sysconfdir@/sysconfsubdir@/sesman.ini
.br
@localstatedir@/log/xrdp\-sesman.log
.br
@localstatedir@/run/xrdp\-sesman.pid
.br
@socketdir@/sesman.socket

